WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.674
Diagnostics make firmware maintainable.

2
00:00:02.754 --> 00:00:06.074
Without reset reasons, counters, fault
logs, health summaries,

3
00:00:06.154 --> 00:00:09.837
and support evidence, teams can struggle
to understand field behavior.

4
00:00:09.917 --> 00:00:14.083
The security goal is to collect enough
information to support the product without

5
00:00:14.163 --> 00:00:17.423
exposing sensitive values or disrupting
real-time operation.

6
00:00:17.503 --> 00:00:21.307
Watchdogs are useful, but a reset alone
may not tell the team what happened.

7
00:00:21.387 --> 00:00:24.345
Firmware can record bounded context such
as reset reason,

8
00:00:24.425 --> 00:00:28.470
task progress markers, error categories,
queue status, version information,

9
00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:29.758
and selected counters.

10
00:00:29.838 --> 00:00:32.494
The data should be useful, small, and safe
to retain.

11
00:00:32.574 --> 00:00:35.472
Fault logs should avoid secrets and
excessive raw input.

12
00:00:35.552 --> 00:00:39.054
A crash or error path is not a reason to
dump every buffer, credential,

13
00:00:39.134 --> 00:00:42.093
key-related value, private identifier, or
command body.

14
00:00:42.173 --> 00:00:46.097
Logging policy should define what may be
recorded and how logs are protected,

15
00:00:46.177 --> 00:00:47.385
exported, and cleared.

16
00:00:47.465 --> 00:00:49.759
Telemetry must balance support and
privacy.

17
00:00:49.839 --> 00:00:53.522
Devices may report health summaries,
update status, error categories,

18
00:00:53.602 --> 00:00:54.930
or performance counters.

19
00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:57.908
Those records should avoid unnecessary
sensitive data,

20
00:00:57.988 --> 00:01:00.041
respect product privacy expectations,

21
00:01:00.121 --> 00:01:04.106
and remain bounded so telemetry does not
destabilize the device under stress.

22
00:01:04.186 --> 00:01:06.299
Field diagnostics should be controlled.

23
00:01:06.379 --> 00:01:09.096
Service tools, support commands,
diagnostic modes,

24
00:01:09.176 --> 00:01:10.987
and trace settings can be valuable,

25
00:01:11.067 --> 00:01:13.965
but production access should follow
documented policy.

26
00:01:14.045 --> 00:01:18.271
Teams should know what support personnel
can see or change and how those actions are

27
00:01:18.351 --> 00:01:18.895
recorded.

28
00:01:18.975 --> 00:01:22.174
Operational readiness connects firmware
evidence to action.

29
00:01:22.254 --> 00:01:25.334
Runbooks, dashboards, release notes,
service procedures,

30
00:01:25.414 --> 00:01:29.640
and incident evidence should use the same
fault categories that firmware records.

31
00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.135
When diagnostics are designed
intentionally,

32
00:01:32.215 --> 00:01:36.260
the device is easier to support without
relying on development-only behavior.
